#N canvas 40 40 1500 800 alert-in 0;
#X text 20 20 alert <level> <source> <text> -- from u_err \, which has already decided this one is allowed on screen. All three fields are cleared first., f 58;
#X obj 20 110 inlet;
#X obj 20 150 list append;
#X obj 20 190 t b l l;
#X obj 820 250 t b b b;
#X obj 820 300 list;
#X obj 980 300 list;
#X obj 1140 300 list;
#X obj 820 340 s \$0-a-level;
#X obj 980 340 s \$0-a-source;
#X obj 1140 340 s \$0-a-text;
#X obj 240 250 list split 1;
#X obj 240 300 t l l;
#X obj 440 350 s \$0-a-level;
#X obj 240 350 list trim;
#X obj 240 390 route warn fail;
#X obj 240 440 2000;
#X obj 340 440 4000;
#X obj 240 490 s \$0-a-ttl;
#X obj 560 300 list split 1;
#X obj 560 350 s \$0-a-source;
#X obj 700 400 s \$0-a-text;
#X obj 20 250 s \$0-on-alert;
#X text 20 570 THE list trim IS LOAD-BEARING. list split hands the level out as a one-element list \, and route matches a SELECTOR \, not a list's first element -- so without the trim \, route warn fail rejects every alert and the TTL keeps whatever it had., f 46;
#X text 420 570 The level is stored BEFORE trimming \, because the display wants it as an atom to print and route wants it as a selector to match. Same value \, two shapes \, and Pd will not let one stand in for the other., f 46;
#X text 820 440 warn 2 s \, fail 4 s. Long enough to read one short line \, short enough not to wreck a set. Errors always time out -- a stuck error covering the display mid-performance is the worse failure., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 2 4 0;
#X connect 4 2 5 0;
#X connect 4 1 6 0;
#X connect 4 0 7 0;
#X connect 5 0 8 0;
