#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 15 1 17 0;
#X connect 16 0 18 0;
#X connect 17 0 18 0;
#X connect 19 0 20 0;
#X connect 19 1 21 0;
#X connect 3 0 22 0;
#X restore 1300 420 pd alert-in;
#N canvas 40 40 1500 800 param-in 0;
#X text 20 20 ANY disp name that is not one of the reserved ones is a parameter. That is the whole registration mechanism: m_nano in Phase 4 sends chop-size 43 % and needs no change here at all., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 inlet;
#X obj 20 170 list append;
#X obj 20 210 t b l l;
#X obj 900 270 t b b;
#X obj 900 320 list;
#X obj 1060 320 list;
#X obj 900 360 s \$0-p-name;
#X obj 1060 360 s \$0-p-val;
#X obj 240 270 list split 1;
#X obj 240 320 s \$0-p-name;
#X obj 440 320 t l l l;
#X obj 780 380 list;
#X obj 620 380 list split 1;
#X obj 440 380 list split 1;
#X obj 440 430 makefilename %g;
#X obj 440 480 list append;
#X obj 440 530 s \$0-p-val;
#X obj 20 270 s \$0-on-param;
#X text 20 600 THE UNIT IS CLEARED BEFORE IT IS SET \, and that is not defensive programming -- it is the bug. list split 1 does NOT fire its right outlet when there is nothing left over \, so after chop-size 43 % the message grain 12 would draw as grain 12 % : plausible \, wrong \, and silent. The empty list message box on the first trigger outlet is what stops it., f 58;
#X text 900 470 makefilename %g turns the value into a SYMBOL. packOSC types every atom after the header as s \, and it will not accept a float there. %g also prints 0.4375 and 43 both sensibly., f 46;
#X text 900 600 So disp parameter values are FLOATS. A symbol value would reach makefilename %g and be refused. Nothing sends one today \; mode changes ride the mode bus \, not this one., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 2 4 0;
#X connect 4 1 5 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 8 0;
#X connect 3 1 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 9 1 11 0;
#X connect 11 2 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 16 1;
#X connect 11 1 13 0;
#X connect 13 1 16 1;
#X connect 11 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X connect 3 0 18 0;
#X restore 1600 420 pd param-in;
#X text 20 580 EVERY BRANCH OUT OF route GOES THROUGH list append FIRST. When route matches \, whatever is left over comes out as a MESSAGE \, not a list -- so status v0.2-ready arrives with v0.2-ready as its selector and alert warn u_init x arrives with warn as its selector. Feed that to a trigger and Pd says: can only convert 's' to 'b' or 'a'. list append turns it back into a list., f 58;
#X text 560 580 ref-conventions.md describes this for a remainder that is a SINGLE symbol. It is wider than that: any remainder whose first atom is a symbol arrives as a selector plus arguments. A remainder starting with a float -- in-l 43 dB -- really is a list \, which is why the level chain needs no list append., f 58;
#X text 1100 580 modal-off carries nothing \, so route hands out a bang and it goes straight to the layer logic. It is a reserved name rather than a modal with empty text because clearing a layer and setting one are different operations., f 58;
#X text 1100 700 led IS MATCHED AND DELIBERATELY NOT CONNECTED. The aux button LED is a display surface with its own owner \, g_led \, and it listens on this same bus. Without a branch here every LED request would fall out of the reject outlet and draw as a parameter row called led. Phase 5., f 58;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 1 4 1;
#X connect 3 2 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 3 3 7 0;
#X connect 3 4 8 0;
#X connect 3 5 9 0;
#X connect 3 7 10 0;
#X restore 20 420 pd disp-in;
#X text 180 424 the bus \, the reserved names \, and where a parameter comes from, f 40;
#N canvas 40 40 1500 900 layers 0;
#X text 20 20 ONE delay PER LAYER \, RETRIGGERED. A bang restarts delay \, so a moving control keeps param alive and the timer fires 1.2 s after the LAST message rather than the first., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 r \$0-on-param;
#X obj 20 170 t b b;
#X obj 200 220 1;
#X obj 20 220 del 1200;
#X obj 20 270 0;
#X obj 20 320 s \$0-a-param;
#X obj 500 130 r \$0-on-modal;
#X obj 500 170 t b b;
#X obj 680 220 1;
#X obj 500 220 del 30000;
#X obj 500 270 0;
#X obj 500 320 s \$0-a-modal;
#X obj 980 130 r \$0-on-alert;
#X obj 980 170 t b b;
#X obj 1160 220 1;
#X obj 980 220 del 2000;
#X obj 980 270 0;
#X obj 980 320 s \$0-a-alert;
#X obj 1160 170 r \$0-a-ttl;
#X obj 700 400 r \$0-off-modal;
#X obj 700 440 0;
#X obj 700 480 s \$0-a-modal;
#X text 20 560 home has no entry here. It is always active and never expires -- it is what the priority cascade falls through to \, so there is nothing to switch on or off., f 46;
#X text 400 560 modal's 30 s safety TTL is insurance \, not design. A lost modal-off on a device with no console would otherwise cover the screen until the patch was reloaded., f 46;
#X text 780 560 The alert TTL arrives on \$0-a-ttl BEFORE \$0-on-alert does \, because alert-in's trigger writes the fields before it raises the layer. Setting a delay's time does not start it., f 46;
#X text 1120 560 Retriggering is why the readout follows your hands: each new value pushes the expiry out again \, so the screen clears 1.2 s after you stop rather than 1.2 s after you start., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
