#X obj 340 450 list append;
#X obj 500 410 r \$0-a-text;
#X obj 340 500 s \$0-text;
#X text 20 580 This draws to screen 3 \, the buffer already on show \, rather than the ALERT buffer plan-v02.md suggested. There is nothing underneath to preserve: the frame is cleared and rebuilt from stored state ten times a second \, so when the TTL expires the next frame is the meters again. Buffer 4 has SINCE been proven writable and switchable \, and is deliberately still not used: switching is edge-triggered where every layer here is state-driven \, so a lost setscreen would strand the display. See ref-display.md., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 3 3 0;
#X connect 2 2 5 0;
#X connect 2 1 9 0;
#X connect 2 0 13 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 12 0;
#X connect 13 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 16 0;
#X connect 7 0 6 1;
#X connect 11 0 10 1;
#X connect 15 0 14 1;
#X restore 20 920 pd draw-alert;
#N canvas 40 40 1100 600 draw-modal 0;
#X text 20 20 A modal is sticky: it holds the screen until modal-off \, or until the safety TTL gives up on it. 16px fits about ten characters across 128 px \, which is why the text is one word., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 inlet;
#X obj 20 170 t b b;
#X msg 20 240 2 8 16;
#X obj 20 290 list append;
#X obj 180 250 r \$0-m-text;
#X obj 20 340 s \$0-text;
#X obj 400 240 s \$0-meters-small;
#X text 20 430 Phase 3's caller is u_init \, which shows booting \, wiring and launchpad while the meters have nothing to say. That gives the layer a real caller rather than shipping it untested., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 1 3 0;
#X connect 2 0 7 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 4 1;
#X restore 300 920 pd draw-modal;
#N canvas 40 40 1400 760 draw-param 0;
#X text 20 20 The param layer is now a MOST-RECENTLY-USED LIST of up to five controls \, newest first \, so two hands on two faders both stay readable. How many are moving decides the type size -- one control still gets the 24px it always had., f 58;
#X text 20 130 THE LAYOUT BRANCHES ARE THREE SUBPATCHES \, not three columns on one canvas \, because each owns a whole geometry and they are read one at a time. pd many carries the SIZE \, since it loops that many lines., f 58;
#X obj 20 260 inlet;
#X obj 20 310 t b b;
#X obj 300 370 s \$0-meters-small;
#X obj 20 370 text size \$0-params;
#X obj 20 430 select 1 2;
#N canvas 40 40 900 620 one 0;
#X text 20 20 ONE CONTROL MOVING -- the common case \, and EXACTLY the layout Phase 3 shipped: name at 8px on the top line \, value and unit at 24px under it. Nothing about this case changed \, which is the point., f 52;
#X obj 20 140 inlet;
#X msg 20 190 0;
#X obj 20 240 text get \$0-params;
#X obj 20 290 list split 1;
#X obj 20 340 list split 1;
#X obj 20 400 list prepend 2 0 8;
#X obj 20 450 s \$0-text;
#X obj 300 400 list prepend 2 12 24;
#X obj 300 450 s \$0-text;
#X text 20 510 The stamp is stripped by the FIRST list split \, the name by the second. Neither can fail to fire its right outlet \, because a stored line always holds at least a stamp \, a name and a value -- and list split only goes quiet when the list is EXACTLY the split length., f 52;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 1 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 5 1 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X restore 20 500 pd one;
#N canvas 40 40 1300 700 two 0;
#X text 20 20 TWO MOVING -- a small name over a mid-size value \, twice. NOT two 16px lines: 16px fits about ten characters across 128 px \, so slider-1 43 would clip to slider-1 4 -- a silent failure that looks like a working display. Real v0.3 names are no shorter., f 58;
#X obj 20 140 inlet;
#X obj 20 190 t b b;
#X msg 20 260 0;
#X obj 20 310 text get \$0-params;
#X obj 20 360 list split 1;
#X obj 20 410 list split 1;
#X obj 20 470 list prepend 2 0 8;
#X obj 20 520 s \$0-text;
#X obj 300 470 list prepend 2 8 16;
#X obj 300 520 s \$0-text;
