#X connect 4 0 8 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X restore 580 1010 pd meters-small;
#X text 20 1120 THE DRAW ORDER IS THE TRIGGER ORDER \, rightmost outlet first: turn the info bar off \, clear \, draw the winning layer \, flip. gFlip last is not optional -- draw commands only set pixels in a buffer and nothing reaches the display without it., f 46;
#X text 420 1120 gShowInfoBar goes out on EVERY frame \, not once at load. mother repaints the patch screen after loading a patch and restores the bar \, so a one-off at loadbang gets quietly undone -- ref-display.md \, trap 4., f 46;
#X text 820 1120 The 300 ms delay before the metro starts is the same one the Phase 1 display used: mother is still settling immediately after load \, and there is nothing worth drawing yet anyway., f 46;
#X text 1220 1120 Everything that reaches the screen converges on the single s oscOut here. Everything a frame draws converges on \$0-raw or on pd text-out first \, so the one owner of the screen stays visibly one., f 46;
#X text 20 1250 WHAT IS NOT HERE: any notion of which control moved. A layer holds a name and a value \, and m_nano in Phase 4 will send chop-size 43 % without this file learning anything about a nanoKONTROL. That separation is the point of the m_ layer and it is the one boundary that is genuinely expensive to retrofit., f 58;
#X text 620 1250 TWO CONTROLS MOVED AT ONCE will alternate at the frame rate \, because they share one param slot. Splitting the parameter area into two 16px rows is the fix \, and it wants a real controller to judge -- revisit in Phase 4., f 58;
#X text 1220 1250 A parameter that arrives while a modal is up is still stored and still runs its TTL \; it is simply outranked. If the modal clears with time left \, it appears. The value is current \, so showing it is right., f 58;
#N canvas 30 30 1600 2350 params 0;
#X text 20 20 THE PARAMETER STORE. Up to five controls \, one line each: <frame-stamp> <name> <value> <unit>. The stamp is field 0 and the name is field 1 \, both at fixed positions \; everything after is display-ready and drawn as it stands \, which is why a missing unit needs no placeholder., f 58;
#X text 20 160 ROWS DO NOT MOVE ONCE A CONTROL HAS ONE. A control already on screen is updated IN PLACE -- only its value changes -- and a new one is appended below. An earlier version pushed the most recent to the front \, which is what the plan asked for and was wrong in the hand: two faders moving together swapped places several times a second and could not be read. Positions now change only when a control appears or ages out., f 58;
#X obj 20 360 r \$0-on-param;
